review_text:
Overall not my favourite Yakuza experience, but I still enjoyed it.

Here are my highlights:
- Dragon Spirit;
- The game now includes side stories on top of substories. I liked most of them, and the substories are still pleasant;
- Haruka's story and gameplay will be a hit of miss, but I enjoyed them;
- I did like how all stories tied together;
- I liked how you could choose who to play as during the later chapters;
- The speeches about dreams.

Here are the things I enjoyed a bit less:
- I thought the story was overextending its welcome, especially by the end (although this could also be me blazing through every Yakuza game so fast);
- Shinada. I like the character, but I found that by the time he is introduced, it was hard to yet again "restart" another story (even if I knew it'd
eventually become intertwined with the main story). By that point, you've already played as 4 characters and the story is well-established, with several hooks;
- With so many characters, some gameplay is bound to be less interesting to you, and I found that to be Shinada's case (I liked his fighting style less).

Where's the yakuza? First I was hunting bears in forest and now I'm a teenage girl trying to become an idol... Nothing a little murder and Akiyama couldn't fix! AND we're back to dancing (No, Akiyama, you were supposed to be on my side!!) 

I'll admit, lingering on the hunting is my fault, could've skipped it, but there was no escape from all that dancing ;_; 

Now, Tatsuo Shinada is good enough to land in top favourite characters in Yakuza series. Him, Uno and Kouichi Takasugi are adorable together! I'll keep this part of the game and pretend the idol thing didn't happen.
